{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788130060,"nonce":"U9WZXu1Y52nj1U1zsig/i5GREBQ=","message":"OPEN-QUESTION RESOLUTIONS — 1.4 worker, branch `spec/1.4-rwa-cli`.\n\nThe spec named three. Building it forced five more that downstream specs will\nsee, so those are recorded here too. **None of them changes another spec's\ncontract** — 5.1 (rwtest) and 4.2 (play-loop) were both read before deciding,\nand each resolution defines a surface neither of them has yet specified. Two\nplaces where 4.2 already assumes something (`rwa status`, `rwa watch` reporting\nstatus when watched) are satisfied as written.\n\n\n    --key value        scalar; type guessed: true/false/null, a number, else a string\n    --key              a bare flag is the boolean true\n    --key=value        same as --key value\n    --key:str value    force the type — also :num, :bool, :json\n    --a.b.c value      dotted keys nest:  --until.event.type death\n    --key x --key y    repeating a key builds an array\n    --args-json JSON   the whole args object; '-' is stdin, '@path' a file\n\nOne syntax for every verb, no per-verb table. A per-verb table would be a\nsecond copy of `VerbRegistry` living on the client, and it would be wrong the\nday 3.2 lands. The mod is already strict about types (`VerbArgs` rejects a\nstring where it wanted a number rather than coercing), so a wrong guess is a\nloud `bad-args`, never a silent misread — which is what makes guessing safe\nenough to be the default, with `:str`/`:num`/`:bool`/`:json` as the override.\n\nTwo specific decisions inside that:\n\n- **Values are never split on commas.** `--near 120,130` has to stay the\n  string `\"120,130\"` that `Positions.Resolve` wants, while `--types\n  letter,death` would want an array and `--rect 10,20,5,5` an array of\n  numbers. One rule cannot serve all three, so comma-splitting is out\n  entirely: repetition builds arrays, and `:json` handles the rest.\n- **`--args-json` exists for 5.1.** A test runner wants to send an exact\n  object, not to trust a guesser. Explicit flags override it, so it composes.\n\n\nPretty mode is a **generic JSON tree renderer** with zero verb-specific\nknowledge. Reasons, in order of weight:\n\n1. A per-verb formatter is game semantics on the client, which this spec's own\n   Invariants forbid.\n2. It would silently hide any field a later serializer adds. The generic\n   renderer shows a new field the day it ships.\n3. It would be a third place (after the C# serializer and its rwtest\n   assertions) that has to be updated when a shape changes.\n\nSo the escape valve stays where 2.1 already put it: if a verb wants prose, the\nprose belongs in the verb's own `data` as a digest string, and it renders for\nfree. The client renders no opinion of its own.\n\nThe one exception is the CLI's own domain: `rwa status` prints a liveness\nverdict, because that is a client-side computation nothing in the mod can do.\n\n\"Pretty mode never invents data\" is held literally — no field is dropped,\nrenamed or reordered. The single place anything is elided is the\none-line-per-event journal view, where a long payload is clipped with `…` and\n`--full` turns clipping off.\n\n\n    transcripts/\u003csid\u003e/\n      meta.json          run id, client version, start time\n      log.ndjson         one line per command: op, id, ok, error, elapsed_s, source\n      001-ping/cmd.json     the exact bytes written to commands/\u003cid\u003e.json\n      001-ping/result.json  the exact bytes read back from results/\u003cid\u003e.json\n\n`sid` — not a timestamp, not a uuid — because it is **already** the join key\nfor `journal/\u003csid\u003e.ndjson` and for the `sid` field on every result envelope.\nOne game session gives one transcript, one journal and one set of results that\nline up without a correlation table, which is exactly what a post-mortem (4.1)\nand an rwtest report (5.1) both need. `--run NAME` / `RWA_RUN` overrides it for\na scripted scenario; `RWA_TRANSCRIPTS` moves the root, which defaults to\n`\u003crepo\u003e/transcripts/` (gitignored). A command refused before it was sent gets a\n`log.ndjson` line with `\"sent\": false` and no step directory — there are no\nbytes to replay, but a post-mortem still wants to see the attempt.\n\n`rwa replay \u003crun-dir\u003e` re-sends a run's commands in order, with fresh ids by\ndefault so a replay never overwrites the evidence of the run it came from. That\nis what makes the spec's word \"replayable\" a testable claim.\n\n\n    0  ok\n    1  the mod answered ok:false\n    2  bad usage\n    3  no live bench to talk to\n    4  timed out waiting for a result\n\nThe spec only asked for \"non-zero on ok:false\". The split exists because a\nscript — and 5.1's watchdog especially — needs to tell \"the colony said no\"\nfrom \"there is no colony\", and both from \"I asked wrongly\".\n\n\nA client-side failure is reported in the mod's own envelope shape, with an\n`rwa-`-prefixed code and an extra `\"rwa\"` block:\n\n    rwa-game-down · rwa-timeout · rwa-bad-result · rwa-no-root · rwa-usage · rwa-io\n\nso `jq '.ok'` / `jq '.error.code'` work identically whoever failed and no\npipeline has to branch on the source. This extends the client's vocabulary\nonly; the mod's taxonomy (`Runtime.Err`) is untouched and uses no prefix, so\nthe two can never be confused. A successful round trip prints the mod's\nenvelope **byte for byte** — `state`, `sid`, `ts` included — with no `rwa` key,\nwhich is the tell.\n\n\nThe 0.1 amendment asked for three (no heartbeat / heartbeat with a flat tick\ncounter / healthy). Implementing it against `Poller.cs` split the middle one\nand added the main-menu case, because status.json distinguishes them cleanly:\n\n    down     no status.json, or its ts is older than --stale-secs (10s).\n             The heartbeat is written by the POLLER THREAD, not by frames, so\n             stale means the process is gone. Only this one says how to start\n             the game.\n    menu     fresh heartbeat, snapshot still at its zero value (speed \"\",\n             tick 0) — no game has been loaded this session.\n    stalled  fresh heartbeat, gameLoaded:false over a REAL snapshot —\n             GameComponentUpdate has not run for \u003e5s (Poller.NoGameAfterSeconds).\n    starved  game loaded, fps below --fps-floor (5). The FINDINGS 4b case.\n    ok       live. Paused is normal; the agent owns time.\n\nThe amendment's \"flat tick counter\" turned out to be the wrong discriminator on\nthis bench, and that is worth recording: the bench is paused by default, so a\nflat tick is the *correct* state and would false-positive constantly. `fps` in\nstatus.json is the honest signal, since ticks are frame-bound. `rwa status\n--sample 2` still does the tick-delta observation FINDINGS 4b asks for, and\nsays \"paused — tick will not move without advance\" rather than scoring it.\n\nNote the fps EMA is seeded at 60 and moves 5% per frame, so at 2 fps it takes\nroughly 30s of starvation to fall below the floor. Documented rather than\nworked around.\n\n\nLiveness is checked BEFORE the inbox write, because a command dropped into a\ndead bench's inbox is not a no-op — `Poller.Init` answers it `stale-on-restart`\nat the next launch. But only `down` blocks. A stalled or starved bench still\nhas a live poller thread (that thread is what writes status.json), so it\nconsumes and answers promptly: off-thread verbs succeed, main-thread verbs get\n`no-active-game` straight out of `ScanInbox`. Refusing there would hide the\nmod's own, better answer behind a guess of ours.\n\n\n`advance` gets no wall-clock default. A 60000-tick advance measured 59.9s on\nthis bench (a2.json in the live results dir) and an until-matcher can\nlegitimately run for minutes; a clock would report \"timeout\" for a healthy\nfast-forward. It is guarded by the heartbeat instead — if the bench dies\nmid-advance the wait ends in `rwa-game-down`, with the note that the command\nwill surface as `stale-on-restart`. `--timeout 0` extends that policy to any\nop; `--timeout N` overrides both ways.\n\n\nNo second bench-path variable, per the 0.1 amendment. The protocol root is\nderived from a candidate list and the first one holding a `status.json` wins:\n\n    \u003cvault\u003e/_RimWorld-Agent/config/unity3d/…/AutoRimmer   XDG isolation (this box)\n    \u003cvault\u003e/_RimWorld-Agent/SaveData/AutoRimmer           -savedatafolder layout\n    ~/.config/unity3d/…/AutoRimmer                        un-isolated linux\n    ~/AppData/LocalLow/…/AutoRimmer  (+ $USERPROFILE)     the windows bench\n\n`rwa root --json` prints every candidate and whether it exists, so a wrong\nanswer is diagnosable in one command. `RWA_ROOT` / `--root` overrides the whole\nsearch — that is what the self-test's synthetic root uses, and what a bench\noutside the vault would use. It is a protocol-root override, not a second\nspelling of the bench path.\n\n\nDefault is a direct read of `journal/\u003csid\u003e.ndjson`; `--verb` sends the protocol\n`journal` op instead. `JournalVerbs` runs off the main thread and reads the same\nfile, so the round trip buys no consistency — while the direct read costs the\ngame nothing, needs no live bench, and is the only path that works for a\npost-mortem after the game has exited. The `data` shape is identical either\nway (`file`, `count`, `truncated`, `last_seq`, `events`) so a jq pipeline does\nnot care; `.rwa.source` says which path produced it. Unparseable lines are\nskipped exactly as the verb skips them, and the count of them is reported in\nthe `rwa` block rather than in `data`, so `data` stays byte-comparable with the\nverb's.","files":null}]}